(1427181, 179056, 31211, '<td valign="top">Tristalisk:<br/>Thank you for your comment (i''ve been waiting for a critique from DPC critique club for ages!). <br/>Although i''m thrilled by your critique, i feel you have been way too kind :) <br/>I realize there are some flaws, namely composition and bg lighting. Then again, I took this shot in extremely adverse conditions: 2 paper sheets (from my printer) placed on the ground outside (so I could have direct sun light - couldnt get rid of the shadows, no matter what angle) on a somewhat windy day! LOL <br/>By the way, the straw was inittialy yellow (a vomit kind of yellow) and later turned green, by changing the hue on the yellow channel. The black spot near the straw is a bit of dirt (or maybe some pooh from the ants passing by) that glued to the already wet paper (I would have obviously removed it if it werent for the basic rules)... <br/>Once again, thank you for your time.<br/>M.<br/> <br/>edit - me and my spelling...<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-06-01 21:40:45.</i></td>', 'Tristalisk:Thank you for your comment (i''ve been waiting for a critique from DPC critique club for ages!). Although i''m thrilled by your critique, i feel you have been way too kind :) I realize there are some flaws, namely composition and bg lighting. Then again, I took this shot in extremely adverse conditions: 2 paper sheets (from my printer) placed on the ground outside (so I could have direct sun light - couldnt get rid of the shadows, no matter what angle) on a somewhat windy day! LOL By the way, the straw was inittialy yellow (a vomit kind of yellow) and later turned green, by changing the hue on the yellow channel. The black spot near the straw is a bit of dirt (or maybe some pooh from the ants passing by) that glued to the already wet paper (I would have obviously removed it if it werent for the basic rules)... Once again, thank you for your time.M. edit - me and my spelling...', '2005-06-01 19:15:44.000000', '2005-06-01 22:40:45.000000', 0),
(1428635, 177646, 23458, '<td valign="top">Disclaimer: *I didn''t vote* (the apple challenge just didn''t appeal to me). I''d say the reason you didn''t do brilliantly - i.e the fifteen ones would have come from people who were sick of seeing the margritte imitiations. There were quite a few of them in this challenge, and there have been quite a few of them in other challenges in this site. <br/> <br/>While not very original, it was a very well done shot in the technical sense</td>', 'Disclaimer: *I didn''t vote* (the apple challenge just didn''t appeal to me). I''d say the reason you didn''t do brilliantly - i.e the fifteen ones would have come from people who were sick of seeing the margritte imitiations. There were quite a few of them in this challenge, and there have been quite a few of them in other challenges in this site.  While not very original, it was a very well done shot in the technical sense', '2005-06-02 07:41:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1428725, 177789, 23458, '<td valign="top">I thought I''d actually have a look at some of your photos after all that...<br/> <br/>I like this a lot, certainly it''s one of the better entries in the challenge. While it might not have told a story to the same extent (and thus might not have done as well), I think this may have been better aesthetically without the glass. With or without its a really great shot.</td>', 'I thought I''d actually have a look at some of your photos after all that... I like this a lot, certainly it''s one of the better entries in the challenge. While it might not have told a story to the same extent (and thus might not have done as well), I think this may have been better aesthetically without the glass. With or without its a really great shot.', '2005-06-02 09:25:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(1432513, 177968, 37334, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critique club!<br/> <br/>This is a simple but interesting still life.<br/> <br/>I really like the composition: The repeating pattern of the wedges and the finger holes of the slicer creates a pleasing and graphic image, that keeps the eye moving around the image.<br/> <br/>The yellow and red of the apple works well against the white of the background and the slicer.<br/> <br/>I would have loved to have seen more detail and texture in the apple: Your shallow depth of field has resulted in only one of the slices being in sharp focus. By using a much smaller aperture you would have brought all the wedges and their juicy texture into sharp focus, creating  more impact and possibly a higher score.<br/> <br/>Your wide open aperture has also led to the left side of the image being very high key, so that you have lost some of the detail on the left wedge. In contrast the right hand side is left looking grey in comparison. Experimenting with different apertures and lighting set-ups will help you determine the best settings for your images.</td>', 'Hello from the critique club! This is a simple but interesting still life. I really like the composition: The repeating pattern of the wedges and the finger holes of the slicer creates a pleasing and graphic image, that keeps the eye moving around the image. The yellow and red of the apple works well against the white of the background and the slicer. I would have loved to have seen more detail and texture in the apple: Your shallow depth of field has resulted in only one of the slices being in sharp focus. By using a much smaller aperture you would have brought all the wedges and their juicy texture into sharp focus, creating  more impact and possibly a higher score. Your wide open aperture has also led to the left side of the image being very high key, so that you have lost some of the detail on the left wedge. In contrast the right hand side is left looking grey in comparison. Experimenting with different apertures and lighting set-ups will help you determine the best settings for your images.', '2005-06-04 03:00:18.000000', NULL, 0),
